  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 328 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nSunday storms chances remain HIGHLY uncertain. Of course, if any\nstorms can make it into southern WI Saturday afternoon or night\nthen the outflow location would set up as a front and help \nfocus additional storms there. Since Sat storms are pretty\nunlikely, then Sunday will be very warm and humid across\nsouthern WI. We are talking about heat indices around 100\n(unless clouds hang around). The area will be largely capped,\nbut a shortwave is expected to cross WI during the late\nafternoon and evening. This could be strong enough to trigger\nstorms on the northern periphery of the cap. Therefore, we are\ncarrying highest pops (up to 45%) toward central and east\ncentral WI Sunday evening and overnight. \n\nIf storms develop just to our north (west central WI) Sunday\nafternoon, the storm motion and outflow would usher them\nsoutheastward through southern WI. With our high instability\n(greater than 3000 j/kg) and decent shear (40 kt) that is fairly\nunidirectional, severe wind is our main threat. However, hail\nand a tornado cannot be ruled out. \n\nIf the afternoon storms do not develop, then we could see a\nround of storms develop on the nose of the low level jet\novernight that is expected to lean into southern WI late. This\nwould give east central and southeast WI the better chance for\nstorms late Sun nt into Monday morning (currently carrying 35% \npops). Any outflow from this potential storm complex would be\nthe focal point for initiation Monday afternoon/evening (may or\nmay not be in southern WI). These would also have a severe\npotential given the warm and humid conditions.\n\nMonday heat currently holds a higher chance to achieve heat\nadvisory levels (100-104 heat index), but a lot can go wrong\nwith that if storms or lingering clouds get in the way. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday, northerly winds help temper our heat and\nprecip chances. This is due to high pressure building into the\nUpper Great Lakes. That central US ridge will push back into\nsouthern WI on Thursday. This will nudge the heat and humidity\nback into the area, along with chances for storms. \n\nCronce",
